# Line endings.
#
# oxfmt has no endOfLine option and always expects LF. Without this file,
# core.autocrlf=true checks the tree out as CRLF on Windows and
# `oxfmt --check .` fails on every file it reads — which is why `npm run check`
# could not pass on Patrick's machine for the whole of Epic 1.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout gets LF in the
# working tree, not just in the index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely need CRLF to run on Windows.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Never touched, never diffed.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary

# Text, but generated — keep it out of diffs and review noise.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated=true

# Note on the vendored BMAD trees (.claude/, .agents/, _bmad/): they are
# tracked verbatim and are covered by the `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above.
# Four CSVs in them carried CRLF in the index and were renormalized to LF by
# this change. That is a line-ending change only — no content was edited.
# Marking these paths `-text` was tried and rejected: it tells git the files
# are binary, which makes it want to store the working-tree CRLF for the whole
# tree and stages thousands of files.
